ons rather to  undermine them because we will need them  ourselves by 2010 or so when we are no  longer to have our way as often as we  get it today let me focus on the three  reasons that I think American primacy  will be short-lived first the rise of  Europe and integrating European Union as  a political counterweight to the United  States  second the erosion of what I would call  liberal internationalism in the United  States that is a move away from a belief  in rural and governance through  compromise and institutions to both  unilateral list and neo isolationist  extremes and that will expedite the  erosion of American primacy because it  will alienate other centers of power  through unilateralism through a  stiff-necked gold alone attitude and at  the same time we are going to be  retrenching from certain parts of the  world pulling out Neal isolationist  instincts which will effectively  diminish our control over certain parts  of the world and then the third argument  that I want to discuss with you has to  do with the rise of the digital era and  the extent to which contra to Francis  Fukuyama and others we are not really  seeing the end of history the arrival at  some final end stage we are seeing the  close of the industrial era the opening  of the digital era and that is a  fundamental shift in the underlying mode  of production that rather than bringing  us to some stable end state is really  the opening of a new uncertain and  uncharted historical era which I think  will profoundly change our political and  geopolitical lives so let me take five  minutes or so to expand on those three  themes the rise of Europe the erosion of  liberal internationalism in the United  States and the onset of the digital era  and what some of its implications might  be the perspective that I bring to the  table and thinking about Europe is a  conceptual and historical one and that I  share the perspective of many in this  city that if you take a snapshot of  Europe today